{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 24, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a masterfully crafted film that captures the birth of facebook and the turbulent personal and legal battles that surrounded its creation. directed by david fincher and written by aaron sorkin, the movie is a riveting blend of drama, tension, and sharp dialogue that feels as fast-paced as the digital world it portrays. jesse eisenberg delivers a career-defining performance as mark zuckerberg, portraying him as brilliant, socially awkward, and emotionally detached—a genius driven by ambition and a deep-seated need for validation. his portrayal is chillingly accurate, making zuckerberg both fascinating and frustrating to watch.\n\nthe supporting cast is equally strong, with andrew garfield as eduardo saverin, the loyal friend betrayed, and jesse plemons as the pragmatic sean parker, whose charisma and reckless energy fuel the company’s meteoric rise. the chemistry between the actors, especially in the courtroom scenes, is electric. sorkin’s screenplay is a triumph—dense with wit, layered with subtext, and delivered at a breakneck pace that never feels overwhelming. every line feels intentional, every pause loaded with meaning.\n\ndavid fincher’s direction is precise and atmospheric. the cold, blue-toned cinematography mirrors the emotional distance between characters, while trent reznor and atticus ross’s haunting, electronic score adds an undercurrent of unease that lingers long after the credits roll. the film’s structure, framed by two depositions, cleverly weaves past and present, revealing the cost of success and the erosion of friendship.\n\nbeyond its narrative, the movie raises profound questions about ownership, innovation, and the price of fame in the digital age. it doesn’t glorify zuckerberg; instead, it presents him as a product of his time—a young man who built a global empire on the back of betrayal, legal loopholes, and a deep misunderstanding of human connection.\n\nthe social network is more than a biopic—it’s a modern tragedy. it’s about how technology reshapes relationships, how ambition can isolate, and how the pursuit of connection can lead to profound loneliness. it’s gripping, intelligent, and emotionally resonant. even years after its release, it remains one of the most compelling films about the internet era, and a benchmark for how to turn real-life drama into cinematic gold.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.00012754580529872328}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -1.5497195136049413e-06}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -1.0728830375228426e-06}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -1.7881377516459906e-06}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.0003810394846368581}, {"token": " master", "logprob": -0.4176788032054901}, {"token": "fully", "logprob": -0.37157994508743286}, {"token": " crafted", "logprob": -0.0009815642843022943}, {"token": " film", "logprob": -0.0058195204474031925}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -0.00013195598148740828}, {"token": " captures", "logprob": -1.2010457515716553}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -1.597391747054644e-05}, {"token": " birth", "logprob": -1.6119593381881714}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.001328777172602713}, {"token": " facebook", "logprob": -0.0006693744799122214}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.8260455131530762}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -1.3232143828645349e-05}, {"token": " turbulent", "logprob": -0.7471824884414673}, {"token": " personal", "logprob": -0.20428036153316498}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.06638368219137192}, {"token": " legal", "logprob": -0.007715780287981033}, {"token": " battles", "logprob": -0.06647693365812302}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -0.20438891649246216}, {"token": " surrounded", "logprob": -0.6067489981651306}, {"token": " its", "logprob": -0.2519676685333252}, {"token": " creation", "logprob": -0.3520713150501251}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " directed